    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah, exactly. standard Kaiming/Xavier doesn't cut it when you're trying to hit the stability thresholds we were aiming for in the '17-18 era. we actually moved away from layer norm entirely for the initial passes and used a localized variance-stabilization coefficient that we didn't publish. i'm trying to see if the current transformer architectures are just a diluted version of what we actually built, or if they've actually solved the divergence issues we were seeing. from your data, does it look like the current scaling laws are actually hitting the theoretical peak, or do you see signs of the same bottleneck we hit before the NDA scrub?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
